  "raw_label": "appellant_lost",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Sivabalan vs Maheswari on 19 March, 2024 Author: P.Velmurugan Bench: P.Velmurugan C.M.S. IN THE HIGH COURT OF JUDICATURE AT MADRAS Dated: 19.03.2024 Coram: THE HONOURABLE MR.JUSTICE P.VELMURUGAN C.M.S.A.No.24 of 2014 --- Sivabalan Vs. Maheswari Civil Miscellaneous Second Appeal filed under Section Marriage Act, read with Section 100 of the Code of Civil Procedure against the judgment and decree dated 29.01.2014 in C.M.A.No.36 of the file of the I Additional District Court, Cuddalore, allowing th aside the fair and decretal order dated 13.10.2011 in H.M.O.P.No.60 the file of the Principal Subordinate Court, Cuddalore. For appellant : Mr.R.Gururaj For respondent : No appearance JUDGMENT Civil Miscellaneous Second Appeal is filed against the judgment and decree dated 29.01.2014 in C.M.A.No.36 of 2012 on the file of the I Additional https://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis District Court, Cuddalore, allowing the appeal, setting aside the fair and decretal order dated 01.10.2011 in H.M.O.P.No.60 of 2009 on the file of the Principal Subordinate Court, Cuddalore. Sivabalan vs Maheswari on 19 March, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/159931443/ 1 3.",
    "facts": "The husband has filed H.M.O.P.No.60 of 2009 before the trial Court under Sections 13(1)(i) and 13(i)(ia) of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955, to dissolve the marriage between himself and the respondent herein solemnized on 07.09.2007, stating as follows: The appellant herein married the respondent on 07.09.2007 in Cuddalore and out of their wed-lock, a child was born. The trial Court, on an analysis of the oral and documentary evidence, granted divorce, dissolving the marriage between the appellant and the respondent, against which, the respondent/wife preferred appeal, which was allowed, setting aside the order of the trial Court. On receipt of summons, the respondent/wife filed counter-claim and also filed a petition for restitution of conjugal rights in H.M.O.P.No.62 of 2010. Though the divorce petition filed by the husband was allowed only on the ground of cruelty, however, the learned trial Judge found that the appellant/husband has not proved the ground of adultery. The counter-claim filed by the respondent-wife was dismissed, but she has not filed any appeal as against the same, and therefore, it operates as res-judicata.",
    "arguments": "The respondent/wife has not filed any appeal before the appellate Court challenging the dismissal of the petition filed for restitution of conjugal rights and she had challenged only the order of divorce. Since she has not challenged the dismissal https://www.mhc.tn.gov.in/judis of the petition for restitution of conjugal rights and that the order operates as res-judicata. In support of his submissions, the learned counsel for the appellant relied on a decision of the Supreme Court reported in AIR 1966 SC 1332 (Sheodan Singh Vs. Daryao Kunwar), wherein the Apex Court observed as follows: \"20."
  },
